## 5. Can the `.unwrap()` actually panic?

**No — not for the value it is given.** Full working in
`evidence/panic-analysis.txt`.

Emitted callee (`reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs:44-50`; this file is
byte-identical in both trees):

```rust
impl FontFaceId {
    pub fn new(value: &UStr) -> Result<Self, ValueError> {
        if value.trim() == UString::from("") {
            return Err(ValueError::BlankValue);
        }
        return Ok(Self(value.to_ustring()));
    }
```

The argument `UStr::new(&[102,97,99,101])` is the constant `"face"`
(102=`f`, 97=`a`, 99=`c`, 101=`e`). `UStr::new` is a zero-cost cast
(`runtime/u_string.rs:22-26`). `trim` (`runtime/u_string.rs:70-87`) strips only
the whitespace set `{0x09-0x0D, 0x20, 0x85, 0xA0, 0x1680, 0x2000-0x200A, 0x2028,
0x2029, 0x202F, 0x205F, 0x3000, 0xFEFF}`; none of `0x66,0x61,0x63,0x65` is in it,
so `"face".trim()` is `UString(len 4)`, while `UString::from("")` is
`UString(len 0)` (`runtime/u_string.rs:170-174`); the derived `PartialEq` on
`pub struct UString(Vec<u16>)` (line 15-16) makes the test false. `trim`'s slice
indices are guarded (`start <= end <= len`) and `to_ustring` is a `Vec::to_vec`,
so no step can panic.

The `Err` arm is unreachable **for this argument**, not in general: the same
`FontFaceId::new` returns `Err(ValueError::BlankValue)` for `" "`, which the
sibling `blankRejected()` sample and `test_blank_rejected` exercise. So the fixed
form is a **latent contract defect, not an active crash**.

